# The compositor configuration, in /usr because /etc is delivered only by signed
# confext under ADR-0004 and this is release content, not configuration a
# machine owns. sway is started with --config naming this path, so no search
# order decides which file wins.
#
# Deliberately minimal: this is what a qualification check needs to observe a
# session, not a desktop anyone would use. Keybindings, appearance and
# application launching are the operator's and do not belong in a release
# artifact.
#
# It carries nothing VM-specific. An earlier draft pinned a headless output
# here, which would have made every machine accommodate the harness; the
# headless backend is supplied by the harness as a drop-in instead, on the same
# principle as the console pin and the hostname. A physical workstation has a
# DRM device and must use it.
#
# sway aborts config loading on any error rather than skipping the line -- an
# earlier draft carried a `set` with one argument and the whole session failed
# to start on it. Every line here has to be valid, not merely harmless.

# Publish the session environment into the user manager, from inside sway where
# those variables actually exist.
#
# This was first written as ExecStartPost= on the unit and did not work:
# `systemctl --user import-environment` reads the environment of the process it
# runs in, and WAYLAND_DISPLAY and SWAYSOCK are set by sway for its own children,
# not in the unit's environment. Measured -- "Environment variable
# $WAYLAND_DISPLAY not set, ignoring" -- so every later unit in the session would
# have had to guess at the socket. It has to run here.
exec_always /usr/bin/systemctl --user import-environment WAYLAND_DISPLAY SWAYSOCK XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP

# Tell systemd the session is up, and only after the environment is published so
# nothing ordered after readiness races the import. sway.service is Type=notify,
# so without this the unit never reaches active. A compositor that cannot report
# readiness should not be reported as ready.
exec_always /usr/bin/systemd-notify --ready
